__registerContent("quickrecall/geography/brahmaputra-system", {"notes":{"qr-brahmaputra-system":{"era":"Quick Recall — Geography (Drainage)","significance":["The Brahmaputra is one of the three great Himalayan river systems (with the Ganga and Indus) and a high-frequency Prelims topic","Its source, the names it takes across countries, its tributaries and major dams are commonly tested","It is central to questions on floods, the Sundarbans delta, and India–China–Bangladesh water issues"],"features":[{"key":"Basic Facts","main":"Length: about 2,900 km — one of the longest rivers in Asia.\nSource: the Angsi Glacier near Mount Kailash, in Tibet (China).\nFlows through: Tibet (China) → India (Arunachal Pradesh & Assam) → Bangladesh.","note":"It is one of the three Himalayan river systems: Ganga, Indus, Brahmaputra."},{"key":"Names Along its Course","main":"In Tibet: Yarlung Tsangpo (or Tsangpo).\nIn Arunachal Pradesh: Siang / Dihang.\nIn Assam: Brahmaputra.\nIn Bangladesh: Jamuna (after joining, the combined river with the Ganga/Padma is called Meghna near the mouth).","note":"It joins the Ganga (Padma) near Goalundo in Bangladesh."},{"key":"Mouth & Delta","main":"It joins the Ganga (Padma) in Bangladesh and empties into the Bay of Bengal.\nTogether the Ganga and Brahmaputra form one of the world's largest deltas — the Sundarbans Delta.","note":"The Sundarbans is famous for mangroves and the Royal Bengal Tiger."},{"key":"Major Tributaries","main":"Subansiri — originates in Tibet, enters India in Arunachal Pradesh.\nKameng — rises in Tibet, flows through Arunachal Pradesh.\nManas — rises in Bhutan, flows through Assam.\nSankosh — rises in Bhutan.\nLohit and Dibang — originate in Tibet; Dibang joins the Lohit before entering Assam.","note":"Other tributaries: Dhansiri (South), Kopili, Teesta."},{"key":"Floods, Cities & Dams","main":"Infamous for floods in Assam due to heavy rainfall and its wide, braided basin.\nMajor cities on its banks: Dibrugarh, Jorhat, Tezpur, Guwahati.\nImportant dams: Subansiri (Lower Subansiri) Dam, Kameng Dam.","note":"It is a braided river with large sediment load and shifting channels."}],"traps":[{"label":"Source & names","lines":["Source: Angsi Glacier near Mount Kailash, Tibet (NOT the Mansarovar/Indus source).","Called Yarlung Tsangpo in Tibet; Siang/Dihang in Arunachal Pradesh; Jamuna in Bangladesh."]},{"label":"Length & delta","lines":["Length ~2,900 km; among the longest rivers in Asia.","Ganga + Brahmaputra form the Sundarbans Delta — one of the world's largest."]},{"label":"Tributary origins","lines":["Manas and Sankosh rise in Bhutan.","Subansiri, Kameng, Lohit, Dibang are linked to Tibet/Arunachal Pradesh."]}],"mainsAngle":"The Brahmaputra, as a transboundary river shared by China, India and Bangladesh, is both a lifeline and a source of strategic concern — its floods and sediment shape Assam's ecology and economy, while upstream Chinese dam-building raises questions of water security and the need for cooperative basin management.","mainsTheme":"Transboundary rivers — ecology, floods and water diplomacy","recall":["Source: Angsi Glacier, near Mt. Kailash, Tibet; length ~2,900 km","Names: Yarlung Tsangpo (Tibet) → Siang/Dihang (Arunachal) → Brahmaputra (Assam) → Jamuna (Bangladesh)","Joins Ganga (Padma); forms the Sundarbans Delta (Bay of Bengal)","Tributaries: Subansiri, Kameng, Manas, Sankosh, Lohit, Dibang, Teesta","Cities: Dibrugarh, Jorhat, Tezpur, Guwahati","Dams: Subansiri (Lower), Kameng; infamous for Assam floods"]}},"quizzes":{"qr-brahmaputra-system":{"title":"Brahmaputra River System","questions":[{"q":"The Brahmaputra originates from which glacier?","options":["Gangotri Glacier","Angsi Glacier (near Mount Kailash)","Siachen Glacier","Zemu Glacier"],"correct":1,"explain":"The Brahmaputra originates from the Angsi Glacier near Mount Kailash in Tibet."},{"q":"In Tibet, the Brahmaputra is known as the:","options":["Siang","Yarlung Tsangpo","Jamuna","Padma"],"correct":1,"explain":"In Tibet, the Brahmaputra is called the Yarlung Tsangpo (Tsangpo)."},{"q":["Consider the following countries the Brahmaputra flows through:","I. China (Tibet)","II. India","III. Bangladesh","How many of the above are correct?"],"options":["Only one","Only two","All three","None"],"correct":2,"explain":"The Brahmaputra flows through Tibet (China), India (Arunachal Pradesh & Assam) and Bangladesh."},{"q":"In Arunachal Pradesh, the Brahmaputra is known as the:","options":["Jamuna","Siang / Dihang","Padma","Meghna"],"correct":1,"explain":"On entering India in Arunachal Pradesh, the river is called the Siang (Dihang)."},{"q":"The Brahmaputra and the Ganga together form which delta?","options":["The Godavari Delta","The Sundarbans Delta","The Krishna Delta","The Mahanadi Delta"],"correct":1,"explain":"The Ganga and Brahmaputra form the Sundarbans Delta — one of the world's largest — before emptying into the Bay of Bengal."},{"q":["Consider the following tributaries of the Brahmaputra:","I. Manas","II. Subansiri","III. Kameng","How many of the above are correct?"],"options":["Only one","Only two","All three","None"],"correct":2,"explain":"Manas, Subansiri and Kameng are all tributaries of the Brahmaputra."},{"q":"Which tributaries of the Brahmaputra rise in Bhutan?","options":["Subansiri and Kameng","Manas and Sankosh","Lohit and Dibang","Teesta and Dhansiri"],"correct":1,"explain":"Manas and Sankosh rise in Bhutan before flowing into the Brahmaputra system."},{"q":"The Brahmaputra is infamous for causing severe floods in which Indian state?","options":["West Bengal","Assam","Bihar","Odisha"],"correct":1,"explain":"The Brahmaputra is infamous for devastating floods in Assam, due to heavy rainfall and its wide, braided basin."},{"q":["Match the river name with the region:","I. Jamuna — a) Tibet","II. Yarlung Tsangpo — b) Bangladesh","Select the correct answer:"],"options":["I-b, II-a","I-a, II-b","Both refer to Assam","Neither can be matched"],"correct":0,"explain":"In Bangladesh the river is the Jamuna (b); in Tibet it is the Yarlung Tsangpo (a)."},{"q":"Approximately how long is the Brahmaputra?","options":["1,500 km","2,900 km","3,200 km","4,500 km"],"correct":1,"explain":"The Brahmaputra is about 2,900 km long — one of the longest rivers in Asia."},{"q":["Consider the following cities on the banks of the Brahmaputra:","I. Guwahati","II. Dibrugarh","III. Tezpur","How many of the above are correct?"],"options":["Only one","Only two","All three","None"],"correct":2,"explain":"Guwahati, Dibrugarh, Tezpur (and Jorhat) are major cities on the banks of the Brahmaputra."},{"q":"The Brahmaputra joins which river in Bangladesh before emptying into the Bay of Bengal?","options":["Meghna","Ganga (Padma)","Indus","Teesta"],"correct":1,"explain":"The Brahmaputra joins the Ganga (called the Padma in Bangladesh) before flowing into the Bay of Bengal."},{"q":"The Lower Subansiri and Kameng dams are built on tributaries of which river system?","options":["Ganga","Indus","Brahmaputra","Godavari"],"correct":2,"explain":"The Lower Subansiri and Kameng dams are on tributaries of the Brahmaputra system."},{"q":"Which of the following are the three major Himalayan river systems of North India?","options":["Ganga, Godavari, Krishna","Ganga, Indus, Brahmaputra","Indus, Narmada, Tapi","Brahmaputra, Mahanadi, Kaveri"],"correct":1,"explain":"The three Himalayan (North Indian) river systems are the Ganga, Indus and Brahmaputra."},{"q":"Assertion (A): The Brahmaputra is a braided river with a large sediment load.\nReason (R): It carries heavy rainfall runoff and sediment through a wide basin, causing frequent channel shifts and floods in Assam.\nWhich one of the following is correct?","options":["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true, but R is NOT the correct explanation of A","A is true, but R is false","A is false, but R is true"],"correct":0,"explain":"Both are true and R explains A — heavy sediment and runoff through a wide basin make it braided and flood-prone."},{"q":"The Dibang, a tributary of the Brahmaputra, originates in:","options":["Bhutan","Tibet","Nepal","Myanmar"],"correct":1,"explain":"The Dibang originates in Tibet and joins the Lohit before entering Assam."},{"q":["Consider the following statements about the Brahmaputra:","I. It is longer than the Ganga.","II. It flows through Arunachal Pradesh and Assam in India.","Which of the statements given above is/are correct?"],"options":["I only","II only","Both I and II","Neither I nor II"],"correct":1,"explain":"Only II is correct — the Brahmaputra flows through Arunachal Pradesh and Assam. (Statement I is not a reliable claim; the two are of comparable length and the note does not assert it is longer than the Ganga.)"},{"q":"The Brahmaputra is a transboundary river of strategic concern chiefly because:","options":["It flows only within India","It is shared by China, India and Bangladesh, raising water-security and dam-building issues","It has no tributaries","It never floods"],"correct":1,"explain":"As a transboundary river shared by China, India and Bangladesh, upstream activities (like dam-building) raise water-security and diplomacy concerns."},{"q":"The Subansiri tributary enters India through which state?","options":["Assam","Arunachal Pradesh","Nagaland","Sikkim"],"correct":1,"explain":"The Subansiri originates in Tibet and enters India through Arunachal Pradesh."},{"q":"The Sundarbans Delta, formed by the Ganga-Brahmaputra, is best known for its:","options":["Cold desert ecosystem","Mangrove forests and the Royal Bengal Tiger","Coral reefs","Volcanic islands"],"correct":1,"explain":"The Sundarbans is famous for its mangrove forests and the Royal Bengal Tiger."}]}}});
